# The distance tree: one cone, no trunk, no second canopy.
# Y is up, +Z is the way the creature faces, one world unit is one map tile.
# The lowest vertex sits on y = 0 and the model is centred on x = 0 and z = 0,
# so the game places it by its feet.
#
# Faces are wound so that (c - a) x (b - a) points out of the solid, which is
# what tools/obj2cpp.py bakes as the face normal. Get it backwards and the
# model still draws, it just goes flat ambient dark.
#
# Eyes are not modelled. They are two depth tested dots in the 2D layer, which
# is a pixel each instead of eight triangles.
#
# About 2.45 units tall, 6 triangles.
#
# Under this game's long lens a far tree is barely smaller than a near
# one, so it never stops costing anything and the saving has to come
# out of the model. Six segments, matching tree.obj's canopies, so a
# tree crossing the swap distance changes detail and not shape.
# The base sits on the ground and is left open: the camera never gets
# under it, and a cap would be a third of the budget.

mtllib tree_far.mtl

# canopy, 6 triangles
v 0.000 2.450 0.000
v 0.800 0.000 0.000
v 0.400 0.000 0.693
v -0.400 0.000 0.693
v -0.800 0.000 0.000
v -0.400 0.000 -0.693
v 0.400 0.000 -0.693
usemtl tree_far_leaf
f 1 2 3
f 1 3 4
f 1 4 5
f 1 5 6
f 1 6 7
f 1 7 2
